## Goal
Perform unsupervised dimensionality reduction via principal component analysis (PCA) on gene-level counts to visualise the six samples' relationships and clustering patterns, coloured by treatment condition (untreated vs treated). This reveals whether samples cluster by condition and identifies any outliers.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| `design` | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method

1. Load the gene count matrix (salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv) and design table (rnaseq_bulk_design.csv) using Python in the `gars-bio` conda environment.
2. Log2-transform raw counts (adding 1 pseudocount) to stabilize variance.
3. Z-score normalise each gene across all samples.
4. Run PCA (scikit-learn, n_components=2) on the normalised matrix to compute the first two principal components.
5. Generate a scatter plot with samples as points, axes as PC1 and PC2, and colours assigned by condition: untreated (blue), treated (red).
6. Add sample labels to each point and legend identifying colours by condition.
7. Save the PCA plot as `results/pca_plot.png` (300 dpi).
8. Save PCA coordinates and variance explained as `results/pca_coordinates.tsv` (tab-separated: sample_id, condition, PC1, PC2, pct_var_PC1, pct_var_PC2).

